From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 18:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] New option "--subinterpreter-bytecode" to make modules
 importable by subinterpreters

A new interpreter, e.g. of an "InterpreterPoolExecutor", imports every module
through the standard import system, starting with "encodings" while it is
created. It has no meta path loader of ours, and the frozen table that served
the early modules to the main interpreter was removed after its start, so in
standalone mode no subinterpreter could even be created.

With the new option, the named modules are also embedded as bytecode in the
frozen table, which then stays installed. Subinterpreters import them from
there, through CPython's frozen importer. The main interpreter keeps using the
compiled modules: the meta path loader moves ahead of the frozen importer.
Names that CPython has frozen or built in are skipped, others that are not
included are warned about.

"concurrent.futures.interpreter" is now always included as bytecode: the
pool runs each job by handing its "do_call" to "Interpreter.call", which only
shares Python functions, so compiled the pool could run nothing.

Signed-off-by: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
---
